      {
        "type": "text",
        "content": "Unlike earlier apartheid films like <a href=\"/movie/41949\" class=\"movie-title\" data-tmdb-id=\"41949\">A World Apart</a> (1988), Catch a Fire benefits from post-apartheid perspective, allowing deeper exploration of both sides. The film shares DNA with contemporary South African cinema like <a href=\"/movie/868\" class=\"movie-title\" data-tmdb-id=\"868\">Tsotsi</a> (2005) and <a href=\"/movie/17654\" class=\"movie-title\" data-tmdb-id=\"17654\">District 9</a> (2009), which use genre elements to process national trauma. Robbins' Nic Vos represents the system's human face, reminiscent of complex antagonists in <a href=\"/movie/16635\" class=\"movie-title\" data-tmdb-id=\"16635\">In My Country</a> (2004)."
      }
